---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { pageMeta } from "../data/pages";
import { LINKEDIN } from "../data/site";

const meta = pageMeta("work");
---
<BaseLayout {...meta} active="work" bodyClass={meta.bodyClass}>
  <section class="page-intro" aria-labelledby="work-page-title">
    <h1 id="work-page-title">Robot deployments your team can depend on.</h1>
    <p id="deployment-gaps">Your pilot is working. Then another customer brings a
      different environment, another software version, and another deployment
      to support. Each new site adds another place where versions can drift
      and updates need attention.</p>
    <p>I build the software release, update, and recovery infrastructure that turns those
      moving parts into a process your whole team can run.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button primary-button" href="#start">Discuss your deployment <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
      <a class="text-link" href="#expertise">See how I can help <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
    </div>
    <p class="work-proof">The infrastructure I’ve built has supported <strong>over 100,000 software deliveries</strong> across multiple continents. <a href="#exp">Explore the experience behind it</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section work-story" id="exp" aria-labelledby="experience-title">
    <h2 id="experience-title">From a software change to a customer fleet.</h2>
    <div id="earlier-work" class="work-story-copy">
      <p>At NODE Robotics, I worked on the route from a software change to robots
        running in different customer environments. I built provisioning and
        deployment workflows, connecting releases with feedback from production.</p>
      <p>That infrastructure supported <strong>more than 1,500 production robots</strong>. A hybrid architecture reduced <strong>infrastructure costs by 50%</strong>.</p>
      <p>Scaling a fleet means treating delivery as one connected system: the
        build, the environment, the rollout, and the feedback after software
        reaches the robots. The people running it need to understand each step.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="work-background" id="projects">Building Robotair also taught me to judge infrastructure by what
      customers could adopt, use, and pay for. The wider story takes in
      industrial robotics at Fraunhofer IPA and <a id="current-work" href="/about.html#today">today’s software-factory work</a>. <a href="/about.html#origins">Read my engineering story</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section work-engagements" id="expertise" aria-labelledby="expertise-title">
    <h2 id="expertise-title">Start where you are.</h2>
    <p>For robotics founders, CTOs, and engineering teams in the EU and US.
      I work alongside your engineers, so the system fits your platform
      and the people who operate it.</p>
    <div class="work-choices">
      <article aria-labelledby="assessment-title">
        <h3 id="assessment-title">We need to understand what’s getting in the way.</h3>
        <p><strong>Deployment Readiness Assessment.</strong> A map of your release-to-robot
          workflow, prioritized gaps, and a reviewed roadmap your team can take
          forward. Use it to choose the next investment or brief your own engineers.</p>
      </article>
      <article aria-labelledby="engineering-title">
        <h3 id="engineering-title">We know what needs building.</h3>
        <p><strong>Production deployment engineering.</strong> I build the agreed release
          automation, fleet updates, monitoring, and recovery workflows alongside
          your engineers. You get a working system in your existing environment,
          with documentation and handover so your team can operate it.</p>
      </article>
      <article aria-labelledby="leadership-title">
        <h3 id="leadership-title">We need someone to guide this as we grow.</h3>
        <p><strong>Fractional Robotics DevOps leadership.</strong> Ongoing direction on
          architecture, infrastructure costs, and delivery priorities. I help
          connect customer commitments to realistic engineering milestones,
          with an agreed mandate and clear decision ownership.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="work-scope-note">Every engagement starts with an agreed outcome, scope, timing, and fee.
      An assessment stands on its own; any implementation or ongoing leadership
      is a separate decision.</p>
    <details class="technical-scope work-details" id="faq">
      <summary>
        <span class="scope-summary-copy">
          <span class="scope-summary-title">Practical details</span> <span class="scope-summary-description">Technical scope and working together.</span>
        </span> <span class="scope-summary-action" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="scope-show">View details</span> <span class="scope-hide">Hide details</span> <span class="plus"></span>
        </span>
      </summary>
      <div class="work-details-content">
        <h3>Technical scope</h3>
        <ul class="work-technical-scope">
          <li>ROS / ROS 2 builds, tests, packaging, and release automation.</li>
          <li>Staged over-the-air updates, compatibility checks, and recovery.</li>
          <li>Fleet logs, rollout monitoring, and incident workflows.</li>
          <li>On-premise, hybrid, and cloud infrastructure.</li>
        </ul>
        <article class="faq-item" aria-labelledby="faq-commercials">
          <h3 id="faq-commercials">How do we agree scope and fees?</h3>
          <p>We agree the outcome, scope, access, responsibilities, timing, and fees
            before starting. If the work is already clear, we can scope
            implementation directly.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="faq-item" aria-labelledby="faq-handover">
          <h3 id="faq-handover">Can our team take the work forward?</h3>
          <p>Yes. An assessment gives your engineers a reviewed roadmap they can
            implement themselves. Engineering engagements include documentation
            and handover; we agree any ongoing support separately.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="faq-item" aria-labelledby="faq-robotair">
          <h3 id="faq-robotair">How does this relate to Robotair?</h3>
          <p>I founded Robotair. These engagements are with me directly. For a
            Robotair-specific inquiry, mention the product so we can clarify
            the scope.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="section continue-reading work-contact" id="start" aria-labelledby="deployment-contact-title">
    <div id="next-step">
      <h2 id="deployment-contact-title">Tell me about your next rollout.</h2>
      <p>What do your robots do, how does software reach them today, and where
        does the process get difficult? A short message is enough to start
        a conversation about the right scope.</p>
      <div class="continue-actions">
        <a class="button primary-button" href={LINKEDIN}>Discuss your deployment on LinkedIn <span class="arrow diagonal" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/how-i-work.html">How I work <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="contact-next">Opens my LinkedIn profile.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
